# Illustration

Use illustrations to explain a task or relationship. Adapt the GOV.UK brand’s clarity and inclusive composition principles without adopting its official identity assets or dot-derived graphic device. An illustration is not a device state, a photograph, a chart or a replacement for Home Assistant’s familiar icons.

## Section headers and homepage cards

Use the original header family on the seven section landing pages and the Colour, Data and Illustration guides. Detailed component pages keep their space for rendered examples. Each scene explains its section: typography and swatches for Styles, reusable controls for Components, and a connected task sequence for Patterns.

The Styles, Components and Patterns homepage cards reuse the geometry of their corresponding header. Keep that relationship when either asset changes. Place the breadcrumb, heading and introduction inside one large header panel, with the illustration alongside them on desktop. The panel starts 30 pixels before the reading text and extends to the right edge of the viewport. Use a minimum desktop height of 450 pixels, allowing it to grow with longer or enlarged text. The complete 640 by 480 illustration fills the right-hand area without cropping or distortion. On narrow screens, stack the text and artwork inside the same full-width panel and retain access to section navigation. Do not embed headings in the image. Homepage variants keep their own 320 by 140 composition.

These illustrations repeat their adjacent HTML introduction, so their page images have empty alternatives. The [artwork register](../reference/documentation-artwork.json) records a description for informative reuse, editable sources, the restrained five-colour palette and review evidence. The illustration is never the only explanation or navigation label. Its opaque pale background forms the artwork half of the header, beside the dark blue text panel; it is not recoloured by a household palette toggle.

